#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193766} :activity/id #uuid "baef8b1d-be2f-50d5-a6ff-93120a166f8f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050503 :topic/id #uuid "4e9c2678-4be0-55d7-9838-e4f11d55dccf" :topic/math-academy-id 4021} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193766 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193912 :tutorial/content "Suppose that we wish to find the solution to the inequality\n\n$$\n2x + 6 < 12.\n$$\n\nAn inequality like this one can be solved just like an equation, using the addition and multiplication principles.\n\nFirst, we apply the addition principle, subtracting $6$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+6 & <12 \\\\ 2x+6-6 & <12-6 \\\\ 2x+0 & <6 \\\\ 2x & <6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSecond, we apply the multiplication principle, dividing both sides by $2{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x & <6 \\\\ \\frac{2x}{2} & <\\frac{6}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{2}x}{\\cancel{2}} & <3 \\\\ x & <3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe solution is $x < 3$ In other words, any value less than $3$ satisfies the inequality.\n\nLet's see another example." :tutorial/id #uuid "0d9501dc-3e68-5741-9382-814ea643182b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8062 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "8806810c-20b6-5fe2-8391-54bcad46c9c6" :step/math-academy-id 72622 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193767}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193767 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192842 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192539 :question/id #uuid "ea06319a-98d8-5b73-a224-a8c03005b5db" :question/math-academy-id "e-12876"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5fd17c91-187c-5849-9648-7b31f2b3be96" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048673} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049986}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193304} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193310} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504668} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186504704}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving Linear Inequalities"} :step/id #uu